{"author":{"id":"40e3ba2788b3b9fc163c354afc44e5f9d225bcf0a27e679aaa878dd6135c6dcb"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1781362456,"nonce":"XVdAw7w2Cx9KQZpxqDJM6Q5kIFY=","title":"SECURITY: decompression-bomb DoS -- crafted tiny archive causes multi-second cdir decode","message":"The libFuzzer harness (tests/fuzz/) finds inputs (~147 bytes) that make the central-directory ULTRA decode run 18-30s+ (bounded CPU, not memory-unsafe). A crafted cdir stream expands via long matches far beyond the compressed bytes. A compression-ratio guard was added to the cdir writer (ad923d7) and catches bombs that flush, but the found timeout uses a decode path that spins WITHOUT flushing (neither decompress_block, decode_ht, nor cbuf_flush are the 5M+/s hot loop per instrumentation; gdb attach blocked by ptrace_scope, so the exact loop is unpinned). NEXT: pin the hot loop (perf/valgrind callgrind on the timeout artifact), then bound it -- likely an input-consumption or output-vs-consumed guard reachable from that path, or a per-archive decode-work budget. Also check the FILE-extract path (uc2_extract with a cdir-supplied e-\u003esize) for the same bomb class. Severity: DoS (recoverable CPU), lower than the now-fixed OOB but real for a tool reading untrusted archives. Repro artifacts under the fuzz corpus.","files":null}]}